{"id":"lem-incidence-convolution-inverses-by-recursion","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"f13750dd7069edbc8ea3486b64eba6c7df73a09bbc59110a56b2d401b9078362","item_sha256":"b3facfd486f535e88f3661d7b762abb7a7ece369d091c3db1639eed3c004e859","outcome":"confirmed_nonfatal","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)","at":"2026-08-04","note":"[F1]'s properness clause is true and its proof is two lines from the cited interval definition: for x<z<=y, [z,y] is contained in [x,y] and omits x, since z<=x would contradict x<z; symmetrically [x,z] omits y when z<y. The cited item supplies the interval; the containment is order arithmetic."}
{"id":"cor-inclusion-exclusion-is-boolean-mobius-inversion","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"243c682833dbe2cb57a826fd3d4d9330cb5c00738d79778885faf94b240da33c","item_sha256":"09d4996f0b838823eddcab3db25253e422d4d23eaa5743fe6b4c705579f5530d","outcome":"confirmed_nonfatal","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)","at":"2026-08-04","note":"Step 1.1 leaves two standard facts implicit: cardinality is additive over the finite partition of A_J into trace classes, and the canonical iota carries that finite sum of naturals to the corresponding sum in R. thm-inclusion-exclusion, cited by [L3], uses iota in exactly that additive way. Both are 30-second closures, not holes."}
{"id":"lem-divisibility-poset-is-lower-finite-and-factorises","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","context_sha256":"8e45108842073ad0295d6a86365ce4923ccd87e983f5da45663a6172d9d7e40a","item_sha256":"22ac608ca8b377874544e9388aadba070c61f2f87b4a3fc240e294621b099d06","outcome":"confirmed_nonfatal","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)","at":"2026-08-04","note":"DeepSeek names the fix exactly right: the bound needs [L5] lem-divisibility-via-valuations, which is declared in deps and carried as a Fact in this item; only the step 1.3 tag omits it. Tag incompleteness, not a missing dependency."}
{"id":"thm-ordinal-subtraction","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"9695baf324f609b1d5bf0732163a5a25a5527833208e822950232a08f4762728","item_sha256":"2eb4343275b65dc8d228198427143065d85f5c2c9d3b63fb024cadcc71a42daa","outcome":"confirmed_nonfatal","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)","at":"2026-08-04","note":"The omission is real: no declared dependency proves omega is a limit ordinal, and lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal is the library item that does. It is used only in the Statement's closing aside and the matching Remark about gamma + omega = omega + 1; the theorem and its proof (steps 1.1-3.1) never mention omega. Missing declared dep for an aside on the most standard fact in the ordinal development."}
{"id":"def-first-uncountable-ordinal","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"d136a1d9199ff5d17ffdab368d316acc8aa5cf519edf84adaa404ed34b94d0b5","item_sha256":"e2ee01875fbb631bd6fd1efd78508f4b757dd0833b0f1dcc645fb9d7e715b2db","outcome":"confirmed_nonfatal","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)","at":"2026-08-04","note":"Terra is right that \"no injection into N\" and def-countable's \"uncountable\" are identified through lem-subset-of-countable, not by thm-hartogs alone. But the definition body states that obligation explicitly two paragraphs above the remark (\"That omega_1 deserves the name - that it is uncountable ... - is proved in thm-omega-one-is-the-least-uncountable-ordinal, which is what discharges the naming obligation of this definition\"), so the item does not claim the bridge is free. A remark that overstates one citation while the definition itself books the debt correctly."}
