+{"id":"thm-laurent-cauchy-complete","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"cf72cb0ad7b5762f1d14b7792689e848a473f1fb29fcea5bc9fc6c51998d71cd","outcome":"confirmed_nonfatal","item_sha256":"cc1b09d79aa5ca608eb9ca2a715999b9b4e104e152abd9fcda66a1d06dca64d0","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (gpt-5.6-sol, xhigh; owner-delegated)","at":"2026-08-08T10:32:41Z","note":"Terra identifies a real compression in step 2.2, but it closes well inside the binding thirty-second threshold. The cited def-nat-order states verbatim, 'm \\le n \\iff \\exists k \\in \\mathbb{N}\\ (m + k = n)', and the cited thm-induction-principle supplies induction. Induct on that additive gap k: the zero case is reflexivity, and the successor case composes the induction hypothesis with the already proved consecutive inequality using transitivity from thm-nat-linear-order. No predecessor lemma is needed and no mutation is licensed by R1."}
+{"id":"cex-zero-times-infinity-indeterminate","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"88971447e4b5ec1b6e91a84b63d3fc2707afcb4d086c33a913e8cf697d6d750a","outcome":"confirmed_nonfatal","item_sha256":"c8540f4b61482b0e72b08333b770ac6f4f747c66680519ecc5f909e1b3c40d36","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (gpt-5.6-sol, xhigh; owner-delegated)","at":"2026-08-08T10:32:41Z","note":"Terra is right that the phrase 'converge to +\\infty in the extended sense' lacks a direct def-extended-limits citation. The cited def-divergence-to-infinity states verbatim that a sequence 'diverges to $+\\infty$, written $x_k \\to +\\infty$' under the eventual-threshold condition, and step 2.2 proves exactly that condition. The omitted definitional bridge is immediate, and it is not load-bearing: the two constant product limits 1 and 2 already refute the existence of a single v. This is a minor citation quirk, not a fatal mathematical or dependency failure; R1 licenses no edit."}
+{"id":"cor-convex-subsets-of-rn-are-contractible","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"572661816f4619bae4c4ef7b99ed2d8c453931a7ac4c39f30b27cc6e1de6ee85","outcome":"confirmed_nonfatal","item_sha256":"bb1cce791779ab85da116f724147aed63a1b269df655dfd034a4fbe41580cf38","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (gpt-5.6-sol, xhigh; owner-delegated)","at":"2026-08-08T10:32:41Z","note":"The local Fact shortens the cited theorem's hypotheses, but the corollary does not strengthen them: its own Statement begins verbatim, 'Let $n \\ge 1$. Let $C \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ be nonempty and convex'. That standing hypothesis governs the proof, and thm-two-maps-into-a-convex-set-are-homotopic likewise states verbatim, 'Let $n\\ge1$'. A competent reader restores the omitted phrase in seconds; this is nonfatal restatement compression and R1 licenses no edit."}
+{"id":"lem-straight-line-homotopies-are-continuous","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","context_sha256":"470d396a16c5d98e57808029e09842651f4c2b2ac285e0c760823e2347ab726c","outcome":"confirmed_nonfatal","item_sha256":"4421293444a41a1cc2c640107442e57e67780346c1f2c43db34dfb0274765967","adjudicator":"audit-alpha (gpt-5.6-sol, xhigh; owner-delegated)","at":"2026-08-08T10:32:41Z","note":"Terra correctly observes that the Statement's formal conclusion is continuity, while the title uses 'homotopy'. The item defines verbatim '$H(x,t):=(1-t)f(x)+t g(x)$'; direct substitution gives H(x,0)=f(x) and H(x,1)=g(x), and the downstream cited theorem records those two equalities explicitly. This one-line endpoint check is below the binding thirty-second threshold and the continuity lemma's stated conclusion is fully proved. R1 licenses no edit."}

